WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.880
Sow sixteen plant species in a field instead of one.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.520
Why would that field grow more?

3
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.260
And why would its total change less from year to year?

4
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.940
Start with one plain fact.

5
00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.900
A plot's production is the sum of what each of its species produces.

6
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.900
Every year, ecologists clip the plants, dry them and weigh them.

7
00:00:26.883 --> 00:00:29.543
Grow species A alone, shown in gold.

8
00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:32.463
It makes a tall pile of biomass.

9
00:00:33.803 --> 00:00:35.723
Grow species B alone, in teal.

10
00:00:36.803 --> 00:00:38.003
It makes a short pile.

11
00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:40.707
Now sow them half and half.

12
00:00:41.887 --> 00:00:46.447
If mixing neither helped nor hurt, each species would make half of its yield alone.

13
00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:50.327
That gives the dashed bar, the expected yield.

14
00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:54.407
But the real mixture grows taller than the dashed bar.

15
00:00:55.607 --> 00:00:57.247
That gap is the extra yield.

16
00:00:58.507 --> 00:01:03.287
With an even sowing, the benchmark is the average monoculture, not the best one.

17
00:01:04.547 --> 00:01:07.247
Here the mixture only matches species A grown alone.

18
00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.170
Where could the extra come from?

19
00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:15.270
Perhaps the species use soil and light differently, or help each other.

20
00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:17.990
That is complementarity.

21
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:23.810
Or a richer draw is more likely to include a species that yields highly alone.

22
00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:30.030
If such high-yielding species also gain the most relative to their yield alone, that

23
00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:33.150
is selection. Both can act at once.

24
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:38.793
In a surveyed meadow, a wet patch can raise both species number and biomass.

25
00:01:40.213 --> 00:01:44.713
To separate richness from soil cleanly, ecologists sow richness at random.

26
00:01:45.733 --> 00:01:47.193
Now let the years pass.

27
00:01:48.633 --> 00:01:52.213
Grown alone, one species bounces up and down from year to year.

28
00:01:53.253 --> 00:01:55.513
For its size, its total swings a lot.

29
00:01:56.533 --> 00:02:01.793
Stack four species, each swinging by that same amount, independently of the others.

30
00:02:03.073 --> 00:02:05.253
Their swings do not depend on each other.

31
00:02:06.393 --> 00:02:08.233
The dark outline traces their total.

32
00:02:09.317 --> 00:02:10.577
Here is the question.

33
00:02:11.957 --> 00:02:13.877
Each species swings by the same amount.

34
00:02:15.137 --> 00:02:18.757
Will the total swing four times as much as a single species does?

35
00:02:25.283 --> 00:02:31.963
No. The total swings only about twice as much, while its mean is four times larger.

36
00:02:33.163 --> 00:02:35.823
Good and bad years partly cancel in the sum.

37
00:02:35.823 --> 00:02:38.643
This is statistical averaging.

38
00:02:39.733 --> 00:02:42.733
But cancelling needs the species to swing out of step.

39
00:02:43.953 --> 00:02:47.053
If all four rose and fell together, nothing would cancel.

40
00:02:48.313 --> 00:02:50.553
The total would be no steadier than one species.

41
00:02:51.783 --> 00:02:53.203
So a field is a sum.

42
00:02:54.503 --> 00:02:57.783
Its extra is judged against what its monocultures predict.

43
00:02:59.143 --> 00:03:03.883
When species swing out of step, their swings partly cancel, and that can steady the

44
00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:04.143
sum.
